    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        StudioBid needed to turn an auction API into a clear experience for discovering, selling,
        and bidding on creative work. Visitors should be able to browse without an account, while
        registered users needed access to bidding, listing creation, credits, and profile activity.
        The design had to connect these journeys and make changing information, such as the current
        bid and time remaining, easy to interpret.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Auction interfaces ask users to make decisions with incomplete and changing information.
            A listing may receive a new bid, approach its deadline, or become unavailable while
            someone is viewing it. Users also need to understand how a bid affects their credits
            before they commit. The interface had to communicate these states without making every
            screen feel urgent or crowded.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Make the current bid, time remaining, and listing status easy to scan
            </li>
            <li>
              Show the relationship between a bid amount and the user’s available credits
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep actions and feedback predictable as listing states change
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-src='web-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='465'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid style guide showing the colour palette and Poppins typography scale'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            A restrained visual system for trustworthy auction interactions
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I structured listing cards around the information needed for an initial comparison:
              image, title, current bid, and time remaining. Seller details, descriptions, and bid
              history belong on the listing page, where users are making a more considered decision.
            </li>
            <li>
              The current bid and primary action receive the strongest emphasis on the detail page.
              Credits and bidding history remain visible nearby so users can judge the consequences
              of placing another bid.
            </li>
            <li>
              I defined reusable cards, buttons, badges, fields, and feedback states before applying
              them across the product. This kept browsing, bidding, selling, and profile views
              visually connected without forcing every task into the same layout.
            </li>
            <li>
              Protected and unavailable actions were designed as meaningful states. Instead of
              leaving a disabled control unexplained, the interface tells users when they need to
              sign in, increase their bid, or choose a listing that is still active.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-studiobid-ui-600.webp'
            data-src='web-studiobid-ui-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='513'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid high-fidelity screens showing listing details and account creation on desktop and mobile'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Responsive high-fidelity screens for auction and account flows
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block'>
          <h2>From flow to system</h2>
          <p>
            I began by mapping the main journey from browsing to opening a listing and placing a
            bid. Profile activity introduced another layer: users needed to distinguish between
            auctions they created, items they had bid on, and auctions they had won. The wireframes
            helped turn those relationships into one repeatable dashboard structure.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Kept the browse, listing-detail, and bidding flow short and direct
            </li>
            <li>
              Separated selling activity, active bids, and wins within the profile
            </li>
            <li>
              Reordered content and controls on smaller screens according to task priority
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-studiobid-wireframe-600.webp'
            data-src='web-studiobid-wireframe-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='481'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid profile dashboard wireframe with tabs for active listings, bids, and wins'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Profile dashboard wireframe organising selling and bidding activity
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final design supports browsing, bidding, selling, and account management through one
        consistent interface. Working through the auction states before development helped expose
        conditions that a static happy-path design would miss: expired listings, insufficient
        credits, ownership, authentication, and bids that are no longer high enough. The project
        taught me to consider the whole interaction: what users see before they do something, what
        happens while it is being processed, and whether the result is clear afterwards.
      </p>
    </div>

